1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,125
♪♪

2
00:00:36,403 --> 00:00:38,304
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,409
♪♪

4
00:01:11,838 --> 00:01:14,831
♪♪

5
00:01:57,484 --> 00:01:58,577
Come on!

6
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:17,780
Mi... Mrs. McCardle?
M-Mrs. McCardle?

7
00:02:17,804 --> 00:02:20,216
Mrs. McCardle, where you going?

8
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:22,352
Oh, I'm going to St.
Augustine's to light a candle

9
00:02:22,376 --> 00:02:24,954
for my Uncle Hugh
who died this day.

10
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:25,955
Oh, I'm sorry.

11
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:27,223
You just found out?

12
00:02:27,247 --> 00:02:29,926
Oh, no. He died this
day 18 years ago.

13
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:32,695
Oh. Well, M-M-Mrs. McCardle...

14
00:02:32,719 --> 00:02:33,963
He was a darlin' man.

15
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:35,098
Darlin'.

16
00:02:35,122 --> 00:02:37,266
M-Mrs. McCardle,
some very important

17
00:02:37,290 --> 00:02:38,601
people are honoring me

18
00:02:38,625 --> 00:02:40,903
with a luncheon in Atlanta,
and my car won't start.

19
00:02:40,927 --> 00:02:42,405
I need a lift.

20
00:02:42,429 --> 00:02:45,174
Oh. Well, I'm a Christian
woman, so all right.

21
00:02:45,198 --> 00:02:47,744
But first you'll come
with me to St. Augustine.

22
00:02:47,768 --> 00:02:50,647
Well... well, I'm late already.

23
00:02:50,671 --> 00:02:53,149
Uh... la-later,
I'll-I'll light a candle

24
00:02:53,173 --> 00:02:54,651
for Unc... Uncle Hugh myself.

25
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:57,220
Oh, that's a darlin'
idea, just darlin'.

26
00:02:57,244 --> 00:03:00,408
Good. I'll work on my
speech on the way.

27
00:03:05,485 --> 00:03:07,697
The Chamber of
Commerce's Man of the Year.

28
00:03:07,721 --> 00:03:08,865
Don't you think?

29
00:03:08,889 --> 00:03:10,733
Oh, yeah.

30
00:03:10,757 --> 00:03:13,736
I'm so happy it's Ben.

31
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:15,438
He's quite a representative.

32
00:03:25,939 --> 00:03:27,483
Oh, no.

33
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:28,618
I don't believe this.

34
00:03:28,642 --> 00:03:29,819
How could this happen?

35
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:31,087
What's wrong?

36
00:03:31,111 --> 00:03:33,307
The master of ceremonies.

37
00:03:34,314 --> 00:03:36,059
"Arthur Saxon."

38
00:03:36,083 --> 00:03:37,193
Didn't you date him?

39
00:03:37,217 --> 00:03:38,194
Date?

40
00:03:38,218 --> 00:03:39,796
Oh, he's such a jerk.

41
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:40,897
Jerk?

42
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:42,999
Do you ever listen
to Arthur on the radio?

43
00:03:43,023 --> 00:03:44,033
No.

44
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:45,535
Oh, you're lucky.

45
00:03:45,559 --> 00:03:48,438
The last time... the only time...
I ever had a date with him,

46
00:03:48,462 --> 00:03:49,906
do you know what he said?

47
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:50,907
What?

48
00:03:50,931 --> 00:03:52,909
That I was lucky to be with him.

49
00:03:52,933 --> 00:03:54,544
Oh! Jerk.

50
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:56,312
Oh, man, he
thinks he's so great.

51
00:03:56,336 --> 00:03:57,947
He's insensitive,
he's totally egocentric.

52
00:03:57,971 --> 00:04:01,584
Oh, on a scale of one to
ten, I give myself about a 20.

53
00:04:01,608 --> 00:04:04,153
Hello, Arthur.

54
00:04:04,177 --> 00:04:05,988
Mmm, always
thrilled, aren't you?

55
00:04:06,012 --> 00:04:07,857
Mmm. Hey, listen,
after we get out of here...

56
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:09,692
Uh, Julie March, Arthur Saxon.

57
00:04:09,716 --> 00:04:11,494
Arthur Saxon, Julie March,

58
00:04:11,518 --> 00:04:13,096
the Assistant District Attorney.

59
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:14,397
How do you do?

60
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:17,400
Nice. I'll bet you're
really cute in court.

61
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:19,469
Well, I like to
think I'm effective.

62
00:04:19,493 --> 00:04:20,603
Well, that's what I mean.

63
00:04:20,627 --> 00:04:22,438
That fire in your
eyes? That's adorable.

64
00:04:22,462 --> 00:04:23,706
You don't even like Ben.

65
00:04:23,730 --> 00:04:25,374
Why are you the
master of ceremonies?

66
00:04:25,398 --> 00:04:26,943
Because I'm Atlanta's
number-one celeb.

67
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:28,311
Besides, who else
they gonna ask?

68
00:04:28,335 --> 00:04:30,747
So, where is the geezer?

69
00:04:30,771 --> 00:04:32,081
Do you mean Ben?

70
00:04:32,105 --> 00:04:34,784
Are you gonna say
nice things about Ben?

71
00:04:34,808 --> 00:04:37,039
Baby, I'm never nice.

72
00:04:45,585 --> 00:04:46,796
This is not like Ben.

73
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:50,199
Look, gang, at
3:30, I'm outta here.

74
00:04:50,223 --> 00:04:52,068
I'm starting this, uh,
bash without the geezer.

75
00:04:52,092 --> 00:04:53,136
You can't.

76
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:56,653
Honey, baby, watch me.

77
00:05:09,576 --> 00:05:12,455
Well, that's peculiar,
very peculiar.

78
00:05:12,479 --> 00:05:14,791
What's peculiar?

79
00:05:14,815 --> 00:05:17,341
Mrs. McCardle!

80
00:05:18,718 --> 00:05:21,130
M-Mrs. McCardle,
where... where are we?!

81
00:05:21,154 --> 00:05:23,533
Oh, now, now, I'll
tell you in a minute.

82
00:05:23,557 --> 00:05:25,735
Now let's see. Am I
not seeing the I-20?

83
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:27,270
92.

84
00:05:27,294 --> 00:05:30,273
Ah, there we are, Looperwillow.

85
00:05:30,297 --> 00:05:32,041
Ah, I should have taken exit 91.

86
00:05:32,065 --> 00:05:34,677
That was my mistake,
not taking exit 91.

87
00:05:34,701 --> 00:05:35,745
Get in the car!

88
00:05:35,769 --> 00:05:37,280
I'm driving very fast!

89
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,815
Don't you be looking
at me like that.

90
00:05:51,084 --> 00:05:53,062
Do you have a St.
Christopher's medal?

91
00:05:53,086 --> 00:05:55,865
Of course not!

92
00:05:55,889 --> 00:05:57,967
Well, there you are. That's
why we're having trouble.

93
00:05:57,991 --> 00:05:59,569
Do you have a St.
Christopher's medal?

94
00:05:59,593 --> 00:06:01,337
Of course I do.

95
00:06:01,361 --> 00:06:02,920
Didn't do much for you.

96
00:06:03,964 --> 00:06:05,341
You folks need some help?

97
00:06:05,365 --> 00:06:08,377
Oh, young man,
we have a flat tire.

98
00:06:08,401 --> 00:06:10,346
Uh, uh, do you suppose that
you could change it for us?

99
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:11,347
That's no problem.

100
00:06:11,371 --> 00:06:13,015
You got a spare and a jack?

101
00:06:13,039 --> 00:06:14,530
McCARDLE: Uh, in the trunk.

102
00:06:17,544 --> 00:06:20,673
That's what my St.
Christopher medal did for me.

103
00:06:33,994 --> 00:06:35,037
Wasn't home.

104
00:06:35,061 --> 00:06:37,073
Mrs. McCardle
wasn't there, either.

105
00:06:37,097 --> 00:06:38,708
Maybe I should go over there.

106
00:06:38,732 --> 00:06:40,142
Let's give him
another five minutes.

107
00:06:43,336 --> 00:06:45,481
Ladies and gentlemen,
he's not here yet.

108
00:06:45,505 --> 00:06:47,683
The geezer's
probably gone fishing!

109
00:06:47,707 --> 00:06:51,320
But that's okay,
because what we do have

110
00:06:51,344 --> 00:06:53,589
is his lovely associate,
who just happens to be

111
00:06:53,613 --> 00:06:56,726
the former Miss Legs of the
Universe, Michelle Thomas,

112
00:06:56,750 --> 00:07:00,129
who just happens
to be crazy about me.

113
00:07:05,292 --> 00:07:07,436
I'm not crazy about you.

114
00:07:10,430 --> 00:07:12,141
Working side-by-side

115
00:07:12,165 --> 00:07:14,877
with one of the most incisive
legal minds in the country

116
00:07:14,901 --> 00:07:17,680
has been a tremendous
learning experience for me.

117
00:07:17,704 --> 00:07:19,982
Ben has defended
all kinds of people,

118
00:07:20,006 --> 00:07:22,351
from very wealthy to very poor,

119
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:24,453
from very intelligent to...

120
00:07:24,477 --> 00:07:27,823
Well, there was this one
very sweet young man,

121
00:07:27,847 --> 00:07:28,847
Tommy Jenks.

122
00:07:33,653 --> 00:07:36,555
Sandra? I fixed your horse.

123
00:07:43,129 --> 00:07:45,875
Hey, Tommy.

124
00:07:45,899 --> 00:07:47,390
Tommy?!

125
00:07:48,902 --> 00:07:50,131
What's wrong?!

126
00:07:59,779 --> 00:08:01,524
You come along, now.

127
00:08:01,548 --> 00:08:04,347
Don't give us any trouble.

128
00:08:06,519 --> 00:08:07,646
Come on.

129
00:08:14,527 --> 00:08:15,571
Easy, now.

130
00:08:15,595 --> 00:08:17,540
Put the cuffs on him!

131
00:08:18,598 --> 00:08:19,742
Take it easy, son!

132
00:08:24,904 --> 00:08:27,183
- No, please stop! What are you doing?!
- Tommy!

133
00:08:27,207 --> 00:08:28,484
- Let me go!
- No!

134
00:08:28,508 --> 00:08:30,219
Tommy, stop it!

135
00:08:30,243 --> 00:08:32,542
Now, stop fighting right now!

136
00:08:33,546 --> 00:08:35,691
Now, Tommy, you do

137
00:08:35,715 --> 00:08:38,361
what the policemen
tell you to do, hear?

138
00:08:38,385 --> 00:08:40,062
Yes, sir.

139
00:08:40,086 --> 00:08:41,664
Put your hands behind your back.

140
00:08:41,688 --> 00:08:43,366
<i>Ben's old friend
Sam Taylor suffered</i>

141
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:44,633
<i>a terrible personal tragedy</i>

142
00:08:44,657 --> 00:08:47,103
<i>when his daughter
Sandra was murdered.</i>

143
00:08:47,127 --> 00:08:51,040
I, um, I read the paper.

144
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:52,908
I'm really sorry about Sandra.

145
00:08:52,932 --> 00:08:54,443
Thanks.

146
00:08:54,467 --> 00:08:56,612
Things sure are never
gonna be the same

147
00:08:56,636 --> 00:08:57,813
around here without her.

148
00:08:57,837 --> 00:09:00,649
Is there anything
I can do for you?

149
00:09:00,673 --> 00:09:02,385
Well, yeah, yeah.

150
00:09:02,409 --> 00:09:05,021
The police have
arrested Tommy Jenks.

151
00:09:05,045 --> 00:09:06,422
Yeah, I read about that.

152
00:09:06,446 --> 00:09:07,990
Tommy's retarded.

153
00:09:08,014 --> 00:09:10,860
His father was one
of my best friends.

154
00:09:10,884 --> 00:09:11,994
Can you help, Ben?

155
00:09:12,018 --> 00:09:14,530
It would, it would
mean the world to me.

156
00:09:14,554 --> 00:09:19,635
I knew Miss Sandra was dead
'cause sometimes on the farm,

157
00:09:19,659 --> 00:09:21,270
horses, they get dead.

158
00:09:21,294 --> 00:09:24,240
They get all cold
and they don't move,

159
00:09:24,264 --> 00:09:26,308
and it's just the way she was,

160
00:09:26,332 --> 00:09:31,213
and it made me scared to
see Miss Sandra like that.

161
00:09:31,237 --> 00:09:33,282
Come sit down.

162
00:09:33,306 --> 00:09:34,865
Sit down.

163
00:09:41,714 --> 00:09:45,412
That's why you ran away and hid?

164
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:49,279
Can I go home now?

165
00:09:51,191 --> 00:09:53,936
No. Not... not yet.

166
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:57,873
Oh, I forgot to feed the
horses their oats this morning.

167
00:09:57,897 --> 00:09:59,508
Mr. Clint's gonna be so mad!

168
00:09:59,532 --> 00:10:03,279
Tommy, listen to me.

169
00:10:03,303 --> 00:10:07,550
Some people think
you killed Sandra,

170
00:10:07,574 --> 00:10:11,020
and that means there's
going to have to be a trial.

171
00:10:11,044 --> 00:10:12,254
Are you... are you with me?

172
00:10:12,278 --> 00:10:13,856
Do you understand?

173
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:18,227
I didn't... I didn't clean
out the stalls either.

174
00:10:18,251 --> 00:10:23,099
Tommy, Tommy,
listen to me, listen to me.

175
00:10:23,123 --> 00:10:25,701
We can tell the judge...

176
00:10:25,725 --> 00:10:29,004
He's the man in
charge of the trial...

177
00:10:29,028 --> 00:10:35,010
That you wish to plead
diminished capacity.

178
00:10:35,034 --> 00:10:38,581
Tha-That means that, that
maybe you killed Sandra,

179
00:10:38,605 --> 00:10:41,350
but you didn't know
what you were doing,

180
00:10:41,374 --> 00:10:42,952
and-and you didn't mean to.

181
00:10:42,976 --> 00:10:44,954
That's wrong!

182
00:10:44,978 --> 00:10:47,056
I didn't kill Sandra!

183
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:52,461
But, Tommy, see, sometimes,
sometimes we have...

184
00:10:52,485 --> 00:10:54,663
Oh, Lord.

185
00:10:54,687 --> 00:10:58,100
<i>Ben was convinced Tommy
hadn't murdered Sandra,</i>

186
00:10:58,124 --> 00:11:00,836
<i>and he fought tooth and
nail to prove his innocence.</i>

187
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:02,872
Sam, when you take a walk,

188
00:11:02,896 --> 00:11:05,141
you just go ahead and
take one, don't you?

189
00:11:05,165 --> 00:11:06,675
Two miles out, two miles back.

190
00:11:06,699 --> 00:11:08,210
That's supposed to keep me

191
00:11:08,234 --> 00:11:09,345
from getting
another heart attack.

192
00:11:09,369 --> 00:11:11,247
Oh, yeah, yeah.

193
00:11:11,271 --> 00:11:13,749
What's, uh... what's in there?

194
00:11:13,773 --> 00:11:15,284
It's a grave.

195
00:11:15,308 --> 00:11:18,721
The best horse I ever
had is buried there...

196
00:11:18,745 --> 00:11:20,122
Knight's Legend.

197
00:11:20,146 --> 00:11:21,657
Do you remember him?

198
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:22,992
Oh, that's right.

199
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:24,093
He won the Triple Crown.

200
00:11:24,117 --> 00:11:25,261
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

201
00:11:25,285 --> 00:11:27,296
Broke his leg winning
the Peachtree Stakes

202
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:28,764
about four years ago.

203
00:11:28,788 --> 00:11:30,266
I had to put him down.

204
00:11:30,290 --> 00:11:33,102
Oh... couldn't you,
couldn't you patch him up?

205
00:11:33,126 --> 00:11:35,371
Oh, maybe, maybe not.

206
00:11:35,395 --> 00:11:38,797
But I just couldn't
see him go on like that.

207
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:43,979
Donald, haul a bale of
hay out to the paddock

208
00:11:44,003 --> 00:11:44,980
and break it up.

209
00:11:45,004 --> 00:11:46,248
New mare's out there.

210
00:11:46,272 --> 00:11:48,517
I'll do it, Mr. Clint!

211
00:11:48,541 --> 00:11:49,919
I said Donald.

212
00:11:49,943 --> 00:11:54,123
After all, you're not gonna
be around here much longer.

213
00:11:54,147 --> 00:11:55,658
I'm not going anyplace.

214
00:11:55,682 --> 00:11:57,960
Hell, you're going to prison.

215
00:11:57,984 --> 00:11:59,028
Cut it out, Clint!

216
00:11:59,052 --> 00:12:00,850
No!

217
00:12:06,826 --> 00:12:08,988
What the hell did
you do that for?

218
00:12:15,568 --> 00:12:18,800
Tommy! Tommy, come back here!

219
00:12:22,675 --> 00:12:24,439
What the hell got into him?

220
00:12:26,846 --> 00:12:28,724
<i>Tommy wasn't easy.</i>

221
00:12:28,748 --> 00:12:32,061
<i>At one point, Ben had to send
Tyler Hudson out to find him.</i>

222
00:12:33,486 --> 00:12:35,631
Oh, hey. Whoa, whoa,
where you going?

223
00:12:35,655 --> 00:12:38,067
You guys are teasing me.

224
00:12:38,091 --> 00:12:39,235
No, we're not.

225
00:12:39,259 --> 00:12:40,336
Yes, you are.

226
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:42,004
Oh. Did you hear that, Jed?

227
00:12:42,028 --> 00:12:43,339
Huh?

228
00:12:43,363 --> 00:12:45,140
I think he just
called me a liar.

229
00:12:45,164 --> 00:12:47,943
Well, now... Now,
you got to understand,

230
00:12:47,967 --> 00:12:50,012
L-Lyle here is my friend.

231
00:12:50,036 --> 00:12:52,314
And I don't like it when
people call him a liar.

232
00:12:52,338 --> 00:12:54,250
I don't like it at all!

233
00:12:54,274 --> 00:12:56,485
Whoa! You captured him.

234
00:12:56,509 --> 00:12:58,754
Good work, gentlemen.

235
00:12:58,778 --> 00:13:01,390
Now, speak very softly.

236
00:13:01,414 --> 00:13:03,626
And watch what you say.

237
00:13:03,650 --> 00:13:05,995
And who are you?

238
00:13:06,019 --> 00:13:08,397
I am Dr. Tyler Hudson,

239
00:13:08,421 --> 00:13:11,200
criminal psychiatrist,

240
00:13:11,224 --> 00:13:13,702
and you, gentlemen,
have just captured

241
00:13:13,726 --> 00:13:15,604
a very dangerous man.

242
00:13:15,628 --> 00:13:17,773
Come on. This halfwit?

243
00:13:17,797 --> 00:13:20,709
Hey, let's put it this way.

244
00:13:20,733 --> 00:13:25,481
There were three of us
escorting him to a violent ward.

245
00:13:25,505 --> 00:13:28,384
Somebody said the wrong thing.

246
00:13:28,408 --> 00:13:31,173
I'm the only one still walking.

247
00:13:35,648 --> 00:13:36,659
Tommy?

248
00:13:36,683 --> 00:13:39,461
Mr. Matlock sent me for you.

249
00:13:39,485 --> 00:13:41,477
Come with me, okay?

250
00:13:44,390 --> 00:13:45,868
<i>Eventually, in court,</i>

251
00:13:45,892 --> 00:13:49,672
<i>Ben proved Tommy innocent
with a very unusual tactic.</i>

252
00:13:49,696 --> 00:13:51,140
<i>Now, Mr. Donovan,</i>

253
00:13:51,164 --> 00:13:54,692
have you ever... have
you ever seen this picture?

254
00:13:56,269 --> 00:13:57,430
No.

255
00:13:59,706 --> 00:14:02,618
Don't you think he looks a
little bit like Knight's Legend?

256
00:14:02,642 --> 00:14:04,853
Yeah, I guess.

257
00:14:04,877 --> 00:14:09,191
Yeah. I do. I do. I, um...

258
00:14:09,215 --> 00:14:12,117
I had that picture blown up.

259
00:14:13,252 --> 00:14:16,799
Look at that. Look at that.

260
00:14:16,823 --> 00:14:18,367
Isn't that nice?

261
00:14:18,391 --> 00:14:20,302
I had it blown up and, uh...

262
00:14:20,326 --> 00:14:21,870
you see, right under
the tote board it says

263
00:14:21,894 --> 00:14:25,774
this race was run
March 19 of this year.

264
00:14:25,798 --> 00:14:26,942
Three months ago.

265
00:14:26,966 --> 00:14:29,445
Guess it can't be a picture

266
00:14:29,469 --> 00:14:30,813
of Knight's Legend,
then, can it?

267
00:14:30,837 --> 00:14:32,748
No, no.

268
00:14:32,772 --> 00:14:34,650
No. It could be a picture

269
00:14:34,674 --> 00:14:38,087
of Knight's Legend's
colt, though, couldn't it?

270
00:14:38,111 --> 00:14:40,622
Well, that's impossible.

271
00:14:40,646 --> 00:14:43,225
You heard Mr. Taylor.
Knight's Legend was destroyed

272
00:14:43,249 --> 00:14:45,928
before he could be
used for breeding.

273
00:14:45,952 --> 00:14:48,197
He was supposed to be.

274
00:14:48,221 --> 00:14:51,166
But was he, Mr. Donovan?

275
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:52,801
Yes. I buried him myself.

276
00:14:52,825 --> 00:14:54,236
Where?

277
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:56,872
The edge of the south
pasture where it's fenced off.

278
00:14:56,896 --> 00:15:00,142
That whole area was
dug up this weekend.

279
00:15:00,166 --> 00:15:02,845
There's nothing buried there.

280
00:15:02,869 --> 00:15:07,049
That horse is alive and well.

281
00:15:09,809 --> 00:15:12,608
That's Knight's Legend!

282
00:15:16,716 --> 00:15:18,894
This court will come to order!

283
00:15:18,918 --> 00:15:20,863
Please be seated.

284
00:15:20,887 --> 00:15:23,499
Mr. Matlock, how
long do you intend

285
00:15:23,523 --> 00:15:25,267
to have this animal
in my courtroom?

286
00:15:25,291 --> 00:15:26,769
Your Honor,

287
00:15:26,793 --> 00:15:30,105
this is not a Bengal
tiger or a python snake.

288
00:15:30,129 --> 00:15:33,842
This is just a brown horse
who likes people. See?

289
00:15:33,866 --> 00:15:35,744
Nice horsey.

290
00:15:35,768 --> 00:15:37,479
A few moments, Your Honor.

291
00:15:37,503 --> 00:15:39,314
I'll hold you to that.

292
00:15:39,338 --> 00:15:41,330
Yes, sir.

293
00:15:43,810 --> 00:15:45,521
Mr. Donovan,

294
00:15:45,545 --> 00:15:47,844
perhaps you'd like to
change your testimony.

295
00:15:50,550 --> 00:15:53,629
That is Knight's
Legend, isn't it?

296
00:15:53,653 --> 00:15:55,431
Yes.

297
00:15:55,455 --> 00:15:59,134
And this is a photograph

298
00:15:59,158 --> 00:16:02,595
of Knight's Legend's
offspring, isn't it?

299
00:16:04,831 --> 00:16:06,129
Yes.

300
00:16:08,468 --> 00:16:09,800
You know where
I got this picture?

301
00:16:12,138 --> 00:16:15,050
In Sandra Taylor's briefcase.

302
00:16:15,074 --> 00:16:17,286
I don't know where she
got it. Doesn't matter.

303
00:16:17,310 --> 00:16:20,422
But I am sure the instant
she saw this photograph,

304
00:16:20,446 --> 00:16:24,092
she saw the resemblance
to Knight's Legend.

305
00:16:24,116 --> 00:16:25,894
She showed it
to you, didn't she?

306
00:16:25,918 --> 00:16:28,797
Why would she do that?

307
00:16:28,821 --> 00:16:31,366
When she showed it to you,

308
00:16:31,390 --> 00:16:34,937
you knew it was
only a matter of time

309
00:16:34,961 --> 00:16:36,672
before she put it all together.

310
00:16:36,696 --> 00:16:41,009
And then it would
truly all be over.

311
00:16:41,033 --> 00:16:43,412
So that night,

312
00:16:43,436 --> 00:16:46,014
when Tommy left the bunkhouse,

313
00:16:46,038 --> 00:16:48,283
you put on your work gloves,

314
00:16:48,307 --> 00:16:50,819
took Tommy's knife,

315
00:16:50,843 --> 00:16:54,302
went up to Sandra's
office and killed her.

316
00:16:57,216 --> 00:17:00,596
Working with Ben Matlock
is an honor and a privilege.

317
00:17:00,620 --> 00:17:02,531
I say that not only
because it's true,

318
00:17:02,555 --> 00:17:05,868
but because he said
he'd fire me if I didn't.

319
00:17:08,327 --> 00:17:10,319
Thank you all.

320
00:17:19,472 --> 00:17:21,517
There you go. Good as new.

321
00:17:21,541 --> 00:17:23,101
- Oh, thank you so much.
- Yeah, yeah...

322
00:17:24,143 --> 00:17:25,988
I believe the young
man should be rewarded.

323
00:17:26,012 --> 00:17:27,256
Yeah, yeah.

324
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:31,059
Um, uh, how does
a five spot sound?

325
00:17:31,083 --> 00:17:34,645
That's fine, but everything
you've got sounds better.

326
00:17:45,731 --> 00:17:46,742
Everything.

327
00:17:46,766 --> 00:17:48,076
And the purse.

328
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,100
Oh...

329
00:17:53,639 --> 00:17:54,766
Now, get back.

330
00:18:08,621 --> 00:18:09,731
He's stealing my car.

331
00:18:09,755 --> 00:18:11,900
Isn't there anything we can do?

332
00:18:11,924 --> 00:18:13,835
What can we do?

333
00:18:13,859 --> 00:18:16,226
He's got your St.
Christopher's medal.

334
00:18:18,965 --> 00:18:20,876
Hello.

335
00:18:20,900 --> 00:18:22,344
And now I'd like
to introduce you

336
00:18:22,368 --> 00:18:23,478
to the prettiest
prosecutor in town.

337
00:18:23,502 --> 00:18:25,647
Don't let the title
scare you guys.

338
00:18:25,671 --> 00:18:27,382
She's all woman, all lips,

339
00:18:27,406 --> 00:18:28,750
and a terrific kisser.

340
00:18:28,774 --> 00:18:31,853
Ms. Julie March.

341
00:18:43,322 --> 00:18:46,034
I've never kissed him, honest.

342
00:18:47,226 --> 00:18:48,990
Why did you say that?

343
00:18:50,563 --> 00:18:52,841
I'm here today to talk
about Ben Matlock,

344
00:18:52,865 --> 00:18:55,877
who, up until now,
has always been

345
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:59,348
very punctual and,
uh, very cranky

346
00:18:59,372 --> 00:19:00,916
when other people are late.

347
00:19:00,940 --> 00:19:04,252
But aside from that,
ladies and gentlemen,

348
00:19:04,276 --> 00:19:09,224
Ben Matlock is a man
whose world is his courthouse,

349
00:19:09,248 --> 00:19:12,894
and whose courtroom
happens to be

350
00:19:12,918 --> 00:19:15,731
wherever he finds the truth.

351
00:19:15,755 --> 00:19:18,834
<i>One case took him far from home.</i>

352
00:19:18,858 --> 00:19:21,403
You're not going to get
away with this, Forester.

353
00:19:21,427 --> 00:19:23,572
We printed a
retraction, Mr. Gabriel.

354
00:19:23,596 --> 00:19:26,174
You ruined my life, and you
think that three crummy lines

355
00:19:26,198 --> 00:19:28,276
on page 56 are going
to make everything okay?

356
00:19:28,300 --> 00:19:30,078
You want damages?
Go back to court.

357
00:19:30,102 --> 00:19:31,313
Show Mr. Gabriel the way out.

358
00:19:31,337 --> 00:19:32,314
- No!
- Come on, pal.

359
00:19:32,338 --> 00:19:33,982
You can't ruin a person

360
00:19:34,006 --> 00:19:35,751
and then just throw
them out of your office.

361
00:19:35,775 --> 00:19:37,753
Things have a way of turning
around, and you're going

362
00:19:37,777 --> 00:19:39,721
to get yours, Mr. Forester.

363
00:19:39,745 --> 00:19:41,441
You'll get yours.

364
00:19:59,999 --> 00:20:02,978
<i>This was my first time
as a defense attorney,</i>

365
00:20:03,002 --> 00:20:04,646
<i>and Ben had his reservations.</i>

366
00:20:04,670 --> 00:20:05,581
- Ben?
- Oh, hi.

367
00:20:05,605 --> 00:20:07,783
Oh, hi. You'll never guess.

368
00:20:07,807 --> 00:20:10,686
- Ah...
- I'm taking a leave from the D.A.'s office

369
00:20:10,710 --> 00:20:12,354
and I'm going home to Baltimore,

370
00:20:12,378 --> 00:20:14,990
and I'm going to defend a
client on a murder charge.

371
00:20:15,014 --> 00:20:20,996
Oh, well, uh... Uh, how...

372
00:20:21,020 --> 00:20:23,932
When did this all-all happen?

373
00:20:23,956 --> 00:20:26,201
Well, I got a call today

374
00:20:26,225 --> 00:20:29,438
from an old friend from
high school, Penny Gabriel.

375
00:20:29,462 --> 00:20:31,173
She was my very best friend.

376
00:20:31,197 --> 00:20:34,109
And her brother has been
arrested on a murder charge.

377
00:20:34,133 --> 00:20:36,078
But he didn't do it. He
couldn't have done it,

378
00:20:36,102 --> 00:20:38,113
because he was
always a real sweet kid,

379
00:20:38,137 --> 00:20:40,015
so I told her I would come.

380
00:20:40,039 --> 00:20:41,450
Uh, defending someone

381
00:20:41,474 --> 00:20:45,087
isn't any harder or easier
than prosecuting someone.

382
00:20:45,111 --> 00:20:48,056
It's just... It's different.

383
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:50,058
<i>Hi.</i>

384
00:20:50,082 --> 00:20:52,360
<i>Oh, hi, Julie. How's it going?</i>

385
00:20:52,384 --> 00:20:54,062
Everything is
moving right along.

386
00:20:54,086 --> 00:20:55,230
Oh.

387
00:20:55,254 --> 00:20:56,264
Uh-huh?

388
00:20:56,288 --> 00:20:58,834
You know, Ben,
uh, you were right.

389
00:20:58,858 --> 00:21:01,069
This is... this
is very different.

390
00:21:01,093 --> 00:21:04,172
Uh, I'm used to
having the police

391
00:21:04,196 --> 00:21:06,641
at my disposal for everything...
You know, leg work, and...

392
00:21:06,665 --> 00:21:08,744
All I have to do is
pick up the phone.

393
00:21:08,768 --> 00:21:11,246
Here I am, trying to
do three things at once.

394
00:21:11,270 --> 00:21:14,316
Uh-huh. Well, I
can't do it all, either.

395
00:21:14,340 --> 00:21:15,984
That's why I have
an investigator.

396
00:21:16,008 --> 00:21:18,186
An investigator?

397
00:21:18,210 --> 00:21:20,388
Oh, Ben, that's
just what I need.

398
00:21:20,412 --> 00:21:21,723
Is Conrad busy?

399
00:21:21,747 --> 00:21:23,058
I can... I can ask him.

400
00:21:23,082 --> 00:21:25,060
Well, I'd appreciate that, Ben.

401
00:21:25,084 --> 00:21:27,262
Now-Now-Now,
you don't worry. I'll...

402
00:21:27,286 --> 00:21:29,898
Look, I've got to go, Ben. Bye.

403
00:21:29,922 --> 00:21:31,333
Okay. Bye.

404
00:21:31,357 --> 00:21:33,268
<i>Even long-distance,</i>

405
00:21:33,292 --> 00:21:36,262
<i>Ben's experience
helped me with the case.</i>

406
00:21:37,596 --> 00:21:40,242
I'm sorry, but the car
rental place was jammed.

407
00:21:40,266 --> 00:21:41,825
I'm so s...

408
00:21:45,104 --> 00:21:46,348
Julie?

409
00:21:46,372 --> 00:21:49,467
You're prosecuting this case?

410
00:21:50,943 --> 00:21:55,690
<i>Then Ben helped in a
way I never expected.</i>

411
00:21:55,714 --> 00:21:57,058
Julie?

412
00:21:57,082 --> 00:21:58,693
It's nice to see
you again, Donald.

413
00:21:58,717 --> 00:21:59,761
You look great.

414
00:21:59,785 --> 00:22:01,329
Oh, thank you.

415
00:22:01,353 --> 00:22:02,531
Ben?

416
00:22:02,555 --> 00:22:03,989
Hi.

417
00:22:05,191 --> 00:22:07,435
Conrad's busy,
but I'm not, and I'm

418
00:22:07,459 --> 00:22:09,638
- a pretty good investigator.
- Oh, I...

419
00:22:09,662 --> 00:22:12,207
This is... I didn't...
This is such a surprise.

420
00:22:12,231 --> 00:22:13,542
Julie?

421
00:22:13,566 --> 00:22:15,544
Oh, I'm sorry. Ben Matlock,

422
00:22:15,568 --> 00:22:18,313
this is the
prosecuting attorney,

423
00:22:18,337 --> 00:22:20,348
Donald March.

424
00:22:20,372 --> 00:22:21,550
- Pleased to meet you.
- Ah...

425
00:22:21,574 --> 00:22:24,519
Uh, March? Are you related?

426
00:22:24,543 --> 00:22:26,021
Not anymore.

427
00:22:26,045 --> 00:22:28,390
We used to be married.

428
00:22:28,414 --> 00:22:34,196
<i>Despite his discomfort,
Ben came through.</i>

429
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:35,964
Now, we need to
find out who knew

430
00:22:35,988 --> 00:22:38,867
that Steve was going
to be there that night.

431
00:22:38,891 --> 00:22:39,868
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

432
00:22:39,892 --> 00:22:41,536
- Right.
- Where will you be?

433
00:22:41,560 --> 00:22:42,771
At the courthouse.

434
00:22:42,795 --> 00:22:45,207
Oh, yes. Forester
had a secretary

435
00:22:45,231 --> 00:22:46,474
named Katherine Ellery.

436
00:22:46,498 --> 00:22:49,477
Uh, at, uh... at the courthouse?

437
00:22:49,501 --> 00:22:52,414
Then y-you'll see, uh...
- Donald.
- Yeah.

438
00:22:52,438 --> 00:22:54,249
He's the prosecutor.

439
00:22:54,273 --> 00:22:56,017
Oh, of course. Yeah.

440
00:22:56,041 --> 00:22:57,352
Ben?

441
00:22:57,376 --> 00:23:00,755
Are you asking me if I'm
having dinner with Donald?

442
00:23:00,779 --> 00:23:03,291
Oh, no. No, no, no.

443
00:23:03,315 --> 00:23:04,726
No, it's just...

444
00:23:04,750 --> 00:23:07,028
You see, i-if I find
out something,

445
00:23:07,052 --> 00:23:09,749
I'll want to tell you,
and I wouldn't know...

446
00:23:14,526 --> 00:23:17,339
Excuse me, Mr. McBride,

447
00:23:17,363 --> 00:23:20,141
but what were you doing on
the night Forester was murdered?

448
00:23:20,165 --> 00:23:22,157
I don't believe this.

449
00:23:24,169 --> 00:23:26,815
The man hired me. He
took me under his wing.

450
00:23:26,839 --> 00:23:29,150
He turned me into one of
the best reporters in this city.

451
00:23:29,174 --> 00:23:30,233
You think I killed him?

452
00:23:34,146 --> 00:23:36,224
Hi, Mr. Brady. Ben Matlock.

453
00:23:36,248 --> 00:23:38,994
I don't talk to lawyers.
My lawyers talk to lawyers.

454
00:23:39,018 --> 00:23:40,729
I just want to ask you
a couple of questions.

455
00:23:40,753 --> 00:23:42,430
Sorry. It's lunch
time. I'm out of here.

456
00:23:42,454 --> 00:23:44,833
Uh, well, how... how
about if I go with you?

457
00:23:44,857 --> 00:23:45,934
Have lunch with a lawyer?

458
00:23:45,958 --> 00:23:47,769
No, I don't think so.

459
00:23:47,793 --> 00:23:49,785
My stomach isn't
what it used to be.

460
00:23:52,131 --> 00:23:54,609
<i>Mr. Brady, do you own
your own home free and</i>

461
00:23:54,633 --> 00:23:57,579
clear, or is there
a large mortgage?

462
00:23:57,603 --> 00:23:59,314
Well, it's mortgaged.

463
00:23:59,338 --> 00:24:01,483
Your Honor, for the record,
may I restate my objection

464
00:24:01,507 --> 00:24:02,784
to this entire line
of questioning?

465
00:24:02,808 --> 00:24:03,885
Donald!

466
00:24:03,909 --> 00:24:05,520
Will you let this woman finish

467
00:24:05,544 --> 00:24:06,888
what she's trying to say?

468
00:24:06,912 --> 00:24:08,423
Mr. Matlock,
you're out of order!

469
00:24:08,447 --> 00:24:10,225
Well, how can she
conduct her examination

470
00:24:10,249 --> 00:24:12,494
of this witness if he won't
let her complete a thought?

471
00:24:12,518 --> 00:24:15,163
If you don't sit down,
I'll hold you in contempt!

472
00:24:15,187 --> 00:24:16,464
- Your Honor...
- Your Honor,

473
00:24:16,488 --> 00:24:18,081
may I have a moment, please?

474
00:24:25,931 --> 00:24:28,410
Your Honor, with
the court's permission,

475
00:24:28,434 --> 00:24:32,147
I think it would be in the
best interest of my client

476
00:24:32,171 --> 00:24:34,716
if my co-counsel, Mr. Matlock,

477
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:37,385
would conclude the
examination of this witness.

478
00:24:37,409 --> 00:24:39,587
Mr. March?

479
00:24:39,611 --> 00:24:41,136
No objection.

480
00:24:43,082 --> 00:24:44,392
Mr. Matlock.

481
00:24:44,416 --> 00:24:46,428
Yes, sir.

482
00:24:46,452 --> 00:24:50,486
Uh... a moment, Your Honor.

483
00:24:55,294 --> 00:24:57,639
You've rattled this woman,

484
00:24:57,663 --> 00:24:59,741
and you did it on purpose,

485
00:24:59,765 --> 00:25:01,409
because you want
to win this case.

486
00:25:01,433 --> 00:25:03,111
But if you try it on me,

487
00:25:03,135 --> 00:25:06,105
I'll put this table
on top of you.

488
00:25:08,073 --> 00:25:09,651
Mr. Matlock?

489
00:25:09,675 --> 00:25:11,686
Yes, sir.

490
00:25:11,710 --> 00:25:14,456
Now, the questions...

491
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:19,094
my associate was asking
about your house, your car,

492
00:25:19,118 --> 00:25:21,262
the big checks you're writing,

493
00:25:21,286 --> 00:25:24,466
were meant to show that you'd be

494
00:25:24,490 --> 00:25:27,068
in pretty desperate
financial shape

495
00:25:27,092 --> 00:25:30,038
if the <i>Journal</i> dropped
your column, wouldn't you?

496
00:25:30,062 --> 00:25:32,374
If they drop me, another
newspaper'd pick me up.

497
00:25:32,398 --> 00:25:34,876
But you can't be absolutely
sure of that, can you?

498
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:37,345
Well, no, but, after
all these years...

499
00:25:37,369 --> 00:25:40,115
Abe Forester had decided

500
00:25:40,139 --> 00:25:45,286
that your talent wasn't worth
the lawsuits you provoked

501
00:25:45,310 --> 00:25:48,156
and was about to drop
your column, wasn't he?

502
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:50,091
- No.
- Didn't he say he was?

503
00:25:50,115 --> 00:25:51,493
Well, he didn't mean that.

504
00:25:51,517 --> 00:25:54,562
He-He used to say it at
meetings just to sound tough.

505
00:25:54,586 --> 00:25:56,531
You were in the
building that night.

506
00:25:56,555 --> 00:25:57,966
Three floors up in my office

507
00:25:57,990 --> 00:26:00,168
at a meeting with Skip
Howard and Danny Lloyd.

508
00:26:00,192 --> 00:26:02,070
Started at 8:00, ended at 9:30.

509
00:26:02,094 --> 00:26:04,539
That's right... with Skip
Howard and Danny Lloyd.

510
00:26:04,563 --> 00:26:06,241
- Right!
- But at some point

511
00:26:06,265 --> 00:26:09,310
during the meeting,
you left the room

512
00:26:09,334 --> 00:26:11,679
to get a folder or something.

513
00:26:11,703 --> 00:26:16,017
Were you aware that Danny
was taping that whole meeting?

514
00:26:16,041 --> 00:26:18,119
Danny always had a
tape recorder going.

515
00:26:18,143 --> 00:26:20,455
- It's his way of keeping notes.
- Ah.

516
00:26:20,479 --> 00:26:26,227
Danny, uh, Danny also wore
one of those wristwatches

517
00:26:26,251 --> 00:26:29,364
that beeps every
half hour, didn't he?

518
00:26:29,388 --> 00:26:31,132
Yeah. It drives me crazy.

519
00:26:31,156 --> 00:26:34,035
And if you listen to that tape,

520
00:26:34,059 --> 00:26:39,140
you can hear Danny's watch beep right at...
- 30.

521
00:26:39,164 --> 00:26:40,809
And you know what?

522
00:26:40,833 --> 00:26:46,114
Your voice is not on that tape
for several minutes before 8:30.

523
00:26:46,138 --> 00:26:48,505
You know what that means?

524
00:26:50,008 --> 00:26:52,273
I have no idea.

525
00:26:54,446 --> 00:26:57,492
8:27.

526
00:26:57,516 --> 00:27:01,996
At 8:27... you went
over to the mail chute

527
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:04,489
for interoffice
correspondence...

528
00:27:06,525 --> 00:27:10,538
put this memo in a cylinder,
sent it down to the mailroom,

529
00:27:10,562 --> 00:27:14,932
and that's when the
clerk received it, 8:27.

530
00:27:16,735 --> 00:27:18,813
What do you think, Mr. Brady?

531
00:27:18,837 --> 00:27:22,350
I refuse to answer
any further questions

532
00:27:22,374 --> 00:27:25,503
until such time as my
attorney can be present.

533
00:27:26,612 --> 00:27:28,604
That's a good idea.

534
00:27:30,415 --> 00:27:33,328
In closing, I'd
like to remind Ben,

535
00:27:33,352 --> 00:27:35,396
wherever he is,

536
00:27:35,420 --> 00:27:37,799
that actually, I
did beat him once.

537
00:27:37,823 --> 00:27:41,302
And if I ever get him
back in that bowling alley...

538
00:27:41,326 --> 00:27:43,818
I'll beat him again.

539
00:27:51,503 --> 00:27:53,815
Dumb!

540
00:27:53,839 --> 00:27:55,150
My own dumb fault.

541
00:27:55,174 --> 00:27:57,252
I should never
have let you drive.

542
00:27:57,276 --> 00:27:58,653
I never asked to drive.

543
00:27:58,677 --> 00:28:01,055
If you wanted to
drive, you just say so.

544
00:28:01,079 --> 00:28:02,390
I'm missing my luncheon!

545
00:28:02,414 --> 00:28:03,725
I'm missing a car!

546
00:28:03,749 --> 00:28:05,460
You can always get another car.

547
00:28:05,484 --> 00:28:07,395
Where am I gonna
get another luncheon?

548
00:28:07,419 --> 00:28:10,498
And poor Uncle Hugh is
waiting up there for his candle.

549
00:28:10,522 --> 00:28:12,000
I would've let you drive.

550
00:28:12,024 --> 00:28:13,935
Oh, I'm sorry, Mrs. McCardle.

551
00:28:13,959 --> 00:28:15,870
I don't even like to drive!

552
00:28:15,894 --> 00:28:17,939
Let's not fight.

553
00:28:17,963 --> 00:28:19,574
Don't fight.

554
00:28:20,933 --> 00:28:24,012
- Oh! Ms. McCardle, look, look.
- What?

555
00:28:24,036 --> 00:28:25,647
Look. See?

556
00:28:25,671 --> 00:28:27,282
- Telephone lines!
- Oh!

557
00:28:27,306 --> 00:28:29,450
- That means people. P-Peo...
- Oh. Oh.

558
00:28:29,474 --> 00:28:30,652
Look.

559
00:28:30,676 --> 00:28:33,407
Look. Oh.

560
00:28:47,693 --> 00:28:49,671
- It opened!
- Saints preserve us.

561
00:28:49,695 --> 00:28:52,140
- Uh-huh.
- Oh, Mr. Matlock, wha-what if...

562
00:28:52,164 --> 00:28:55,658
Shh... Shh, shh, shh, shh, shh.

563
00:29:03,875 --> 00:29:07,222
- Ambassador East Hotel.
- Yeah, this is Ben Matlock.

564
00:29:07,246 --> 00:29:08,289
Would you hold, please?

565
00:29:08,313 --> 00:29:10,225
No, no, no! You put me on hold,

566
00:29:10,249 --> 00:29:11,826
I'll find you and I'll break

567
00:29:11,850 --> 00:29:13,928
every bone in your
body, you got that?

568
00:29:13,952 --> 00:29:15,163
Excuse me?

569
00:29:15,187 --> 00:29:16,831
Just listen.

570
00:29:16,855 --> 00:29:19,067
I want you to go into
the West Ballroom,

571
00:29:19,091 --> 00:29:20,868
find Conrad McMasters.

572
00:29:20,892 --> 00:29:23,571
Tell him that Ben Matlock

573
00:29:23,595 --> 00:29:26,975
is in a town called
Looperwillow, Georgia.

574
00:29:26,999 --> 00:29:29,244
I'm going to have
to put you on hold.

575
00:29:29,268 --> 00:29:30,345
No!

576
00:29:33,405 --> 00:29:35,216
He put me on hold.

577
00:29:35,240 --> 00:29:39,120
All right, you
two, turn around...

578
00:29:39,144 --> 00:29:40,855
real slow.

579
00:29:40,879 --> 00:29:44,025
Oh, saints preserve us.

580
00:29:44,049 --> 00:29:47,328
I-I...
- Hang it up.
- I-I...

581
00:29:47,352 --> 00:29:49,297
I know how this must look.

582
00:29:49,321 --> 00:29:51,552
Hang it up.

583
00:29:56,395 --> 00:29:58,606
Ben.

584
00:29:58,630 --> 00:30:00,608
Ben? Hello?

585
00:30:00,632 --> 00:30:02,123
There's nobody here.

586
00:30:14,813 --> 00:30:17,859
I-I'm real sorry
about your door,

587
00:30:17,883 --> 00:30:19,827
but i-it was an emergency.

588
00:30:19,851 --> 00:30:21,863
We just <i>had</i> to use the phone.

589
00:30:21,887 --> 00:30:25,333
You broke... you entered.

590
00:30:25,357 --> 00:30:27,402
Yes, ma'am.

591
00:30:27,426 --> 00:30:30,338
Wh-What are you gonna do to us?

592
00:30:30,362 --> 00:30:33,408
The same thing my family's
been doing with trespassers

593
00:30:33,432 --> 00:30:36,244
for nigh on to a
hundred years now.

594
00:30:36,268 --> 00:30:38,646
McCARDLE: Oh,
saints preserve us.

595
00:30:46,011 --> 00:30:48,489
Ben just tried to
call from a little town

596
00:30:48,513 --> 00:30:50,124
about 25 miles east of here.

597
00:30:50,148 --> 00:30:52,627
I'm gonna go out there.

598
00:30:56,688 --> 00:30:59,367
Geez, wish I could get
out of here that early.

599
00:30:59,391 --> 00:31:01,069
Conrad's gone to look for Ben.

600
00:31:01,093 --> 00:31:03,204
Oh, great... The
geezer's wandered off.

601
00:31:03,228 --> 00:31:05,740
He's lost, you idiot.

602
00:31:05,764 --> 00:31:08,256
And he's not a geezer.

603
00:31:13,004 --> 00:31:14,582
Uh, Sheriff?

604
00:31:14,606 --> 00:31:18,441
These people just broke
in to Mrs. Burnett's place.

605
00:31:19,611 --> 00:31:21,222
We didn't break in.

606
00:31:21,246 --> 00:31:24,559
We jiggled the door handle
a few times, and it opened.

607
00:31:24,583 --> 00:31:26,427
Yeah, well, that
doorjamb's broke.

608
00:31:26,451 --> 00:31:28,329
So you did use force.

609
00:31:28,353 --> 00:31:30,832
We just had to use the phone.

610
00:31:30,856 --> 00:31:33,568
Well, Sheriff, they
got no I.D., no car,

611
00:31:33,592 --> 00:31:35,151
no money, no nothin'.

612
00:31:36,194 --> 00:31:37,905
Transients, huh?

613
00:31:37,929 --> 00:31:41,142
- No.
- We're not transients!

614
00:31:41,166 --> 00:31:43,244
One of the good
citizens of Looperwillow

615
00:31:43,268 --> 00:31:45,413
robbed us and stole her car.

616
00:31:45,437 --> 00:31:46,914
I would've let him drive.

617
00:31:46,938 --> 00:31:48,349
I see.

618
00:31:48,373 --> 00:31:50,785
Uh, just what brought you

619
00:31:50,809 --> 00:31:52,553
to Looperwillow
in the first place?

620
00:31:52,577 --> 00:31:53,621
She did.

621
00:31:53,645 --> 00:31:54,922
I didn't insist on driving.

622
00:31:54,946 --> 00:31:56,457
If he had wanted to drive,

623
00:31:56,481 --> 00:31:58,677
he should've said,
"I want to drive."

624
00:32:07,392 --> 00:32:08,703
Thank you!

625
00:32:08,727 --> 00:32:11,406
Now that I have your
attention, I would like to tell you

626
00:32:11,430 --> 00:32:14,275
how I met the
beautiful Ben Matlock.

627
00:32:14,299 --> 00:32:16,144
My day started as usual,

628
00:32:16,168 --> 00:32:17,879
with my fabulous
number-one show.

629
00:32:17,903 --> 00:32:19,046
You all know my show.

630
00:32:19,070 --> 00:32:20,481
Come on, give it up! Yes!

631
00:32:20,505 --> 00:32:22,183
You know my show!

632
00:32:22,207 --> 00:32:23,785
You're all
beautiful. I love you.

633
00:32:23,809 --> 00:32:24,986
Don't change.

634
00:32:25,010 --> 00:32:28,756
<i>WBW Radio,</i> 98.6 FM, Atlanta.

635
00:32:33,552 --> 00:32:35,296
Anybody out there? Talk to me.

636
00:32:35,320 --> 00:32:36,964
Well, if everybody carried

637
00:32:36,988 --> 00:32:39,634
a sawed-off shotgun,
there'd be a lot less crime.

638
00:32:39,658 --> 00:32:42,136
Yeah, and a lot fewer
people, too, dork.

639
00:32:42,160 --> 00:32:43,237
Whippersnapper!

640
00:32:43,261 --> 00:32:45,139
There ought to be
a law against you.

641
00:32:45,163 --> 00:32:47,275
Yeah, well, there is
a law, mush brain...

642
00:32:47,299 --> 00:32:49,644
It's called freedom of speech,
and it's in the Constitution.

643
00:32:49,668 --> 00:32:50,845
You're out of here.

644
00:32:50,869 --> 00:32:52,580
WBW Radio. Arthur
Saxon. Talk to me.

645
00:32:52,604 --> 00:32:53,663
Arthur...

646
00:32:55,207 --> 00:32:58,986
Whilst we ponder that
often burning question...

647
00:32:59,010 --> 00:33:02,590
What do you say to
God when <i>she</i> sneezes?

648
00:33:02,614 --> 00:33:04,708
Think about it, Atlanta.

649
00:33:13,725 --> 00:33:16,838
<i>Unbelievably, someone
hated me enough</i>

650
00:33:16,862 --> 00:33:18,406
<i>to murder my
archrival Robby Moore</i>

651
00:33:18,430 --> 00:33:20,775
<i>and try to frame
me for the evil deed.</i>

652
00:33:20,799 --> 00:33:24,078
Now, your car was seen coming
out of the Moores' driveway

653
00:33:24,102 --> 00:33:26,147
just at the time Moore
stopped broadcasting.

654
00:33:26,171 --> 00:33:29,217
I haven't been anywhere
in this car since 6:00 a.m.

655
00:33:33,745 --> 00:33:35,423
Still warm.

656
00:33:35,447 --> 00:33:39,026
<i>Ben, a friend of
mine's been arrested.</i>

657
00:33:39,050 --> 00:33:41,496
Well, what was he arrested for?

658
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:42,964
Murder.

659
00:33:42,988 --> 00:33:44,765
Somebody you've
been going out with

660
00:33:44,789 --> 00:33:46,534
was arrested for murder?

661
00:33:46,558 --> 00:33:49,804
Ben, will you
just... Did he do it?

662
00:33:49,828 --> 00:33:52,373
Oh, I can't imagine that he did.

663
00:33:52,397 --> 00:33:56,711
He is such a... gentle...

664
00:33:56,735 --> 00:33:59,080
kind, intelligent person.

665
00:33:59,104 --> 00:34:02,116
Moving toward the
recommendation from both sides,

666
00:34:02,140 --> 00:34:05,920
I hereby set bail for
Mr. Saxon at $150,000.

667
00:34:05,944 --> 00:34:07,622
- Your Honor...
- Pay 'em. I'm out of here.

668
00:34:07,646 --> 00:34:08,723
- Come back here!
- -Arthur?

669
00:34:08,747 --> 00:34:09,691
Bailiff!

670
00:34:09,715 --> 00:34:12,081
- Arthur... please!
- Hold that man!

671
00:34:13,852 --> 00:34:16,130
Man, that judge sure
is a hard-boiled, old...

672
00:34:16,154 --> 00:34:19,834
Try that one more time,
and I'll let you rot in jail.

673
00:34:19,858 --> 00:34:21,602
What's with him?

674
00:34:21,626 --> 00:34:22,803
<i>Of course, I was always</i>

675
00:34:22,827 --> 00:34:24,205
<i>one step ahead
of the old geezer.</i>

676
00:34:24,229 --> 00:34:25,973
Mr. Gilbert?

677
00:34:25,997 --> 00:34:27,275
Ben Matlock.

678
00:34:27,299 --> 00:34:29,277
This is your fault.

679
00:34:29,301 --> 00:34:30,578
If it weren't for you,

680
00:34:30,602 --> 00:34:33,247
that maniac'd be in
jail where he belongs.

681
00:34:33,271 --> 00:34:36,571
This suit cost 800 bucks.

682
00:34:37,609 --> 00:34:39,554
Grape jelly doesn't come out.

683
00:34:39,578 --> 00:34:41,355
<i>So after I'd done
all the legwork,</i>

684
00:34:41,379 --> 00:34:44,725
<i>old Ben finally got it together
long enough to nail the killer</i>

685
00:34:44,749 --> 00:34:46,127
<i>in the courtroom.</i>

686
00:34:46,151 --> 00:34:50,765
Uh... y-you used to
be a... a disc jockey

687
00:34:50,789 --> 00:34:54,101
at, uh, Station WBW,
along with Arthur Saxon

688
00:34:54,125 --> 00:34:56,804
and Robby Moore, is that right?

689
00:34:56,828 --> 00:34:58,339
Yes, I was.

690
00:34:58,363 --> 00:35:01,776
But you were... let go.

691
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:04,445
Fired, Mr. Matlock, yes.

692
00:35:04,469 --> 00:35:08,316
Well... with, uh...

693
00:35:08,340 --> 00:35:11,018
Robby Moore dead and, uh,

694
00:35:11,042 --> 00:35:16,023
Arthur Saxon out of the running,
why, uh, maybe something

695
00:35:16,047 --> 00:35:18,192
will come up for
you back at WBW.

696
00:35:18,216 --> 00:35:21,729
Objection. I-I fail to see
the relevancy of any of this.

697
00:35:21,753 --> 00:35:23,564
Uh, goes to motive, Your Honor.

698
00:35:23,588 --> 00:35:24,699
Your Honor...

699
00:35:24,723 --> 00:35:26,734
the people have already
established motive.

700
00:35:26,758 --> 00:35:28,736
Uh, for my client, yes,

701
00:35:28,760 --> 00:35:31,872
but not for the person who
actually killed Robby Moore.

702
00:35:31,896 --> 00:35:34,208
Am I correct in
assuming you know better

703
00:35:34,232 --> 00:35:36,677
than to make such an
allegation without proof?

704
00:35:36,701 --> 00:35:38,913
Uh, yes, ma'am...
I'm going for that now.

705
00:35:38,937 --> 00:35:40,848
Proceed.

706
00:35:40,872 --> 00:35:42,450
Thank you.

707
00:35:42,474 --> 00:35:46,320
Uh... uh, when you were at WBW,

708
00:35:46,344 --> 00:35:49,824
did you ever, uh, send
your car out to be washed?

709
00:35:49,848 --> 00:35:51,292
Yes.

710
00:35:51,316 --> 00:35:52,526
Where'd he get the key?

711
00:35:52,550 --> 00:35:54,428
I kept a spare in the
glove compartment.

712
00:35:54,452 --> 00:35:58,132
Just like... all
the other people

713
00:35:58,156 --> 00:35:59,567
who worked at the station.

714
00:35:59,591 --> 00:36:03,070
A lot of people did
that, I believe, yes.

715
00:36:03,094 --> 00:36:10,578
A witness testified that
she saw Arthur Saxon's car

716
00:36:10,602 --> 00:36:14,849
racing away from
Robby Moore's house

717
00:36:14,873 --> 00:36:16,817
right after the murder
had been committed.

718
00:36:16,841 --> 00:36:20,521
She didn't see who was driving.

719
00:36:20,545 --> 00:36:23,024
But you knew about the key.

720
00:36:23,048 --> 00:36:25,426
And you knew where
Arthur parked his car.

721
00:36:25,450 --> 00:36:28,863
So I submit it was you

722
00:36:28,887 --> 00:36:31,032
who was driving
the car that morning.

723
00:36:31,056 --> 00:36:34,001
I was at home, sleeping.

724
00:36:34,025 --> 00:36:35,536
Oh, I don't think so.

725
00:36:35,560 --> 00:36:38,739
No... I don't think so.

726
00:36:40,131 --> 00:36:41,776
Well, if everybody carried

727
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:45,146
a sawed-off shotgun,
there'd be a lot less crime.

728
00:36:46,571 --> 00:36:47,748
Know who that was?

729
00:36:47,772 --> 00:36:50,084
I have no idea.

730
00:36:50,108 --> 00:36:54,221
That's one of the
voices on the relief tape

731
00:36:54,245 --> 00:36:56,057
we've heard so much about.

732
00:36:56,081 --> 00:37:00,061
The one the prosecution
says that Arthur was playing

733
00:37:00,085 --> 00:37:02,897
when he was not on the air.

734
00:37:02,921 --> 00:37:05,066
So?

735
00:37:05,090 --> 00:37:06,367
All right.

736
00:37:06,391 --> 00:37:09,136
What about this?

737
00:37:10,662 --> 00:37:12,440
Well, Ma, when we're jawing

738
00:37:12,464 --> 00:37:15,109
with our friends at WCPG,

739
00:37:15,133 --> 00:37:17,812
midnight to 6:00 is
just a spit in the wind.

740
00:37:18,837 --> 00:37:20,948
That's me on WCPG.

741
00:37:20,972 --> 00:37:22,416
That's hardly a secret.

742
00:37:22,440 --> 00:37:24,351
No, no.

743
00:37:24,375 --> 00:37:29,323
The secret is...
that you are also

744
00:37:29,347 --> 00:37:32,727
our shotgun-carrying
Mr. Clemens.

745
00:37:32,751 --> 00:37:33,894
Oh, that's ridiculous.

746
00:37:33,918 --> 00:37:34,918
I am not.

747
00:37:36,955 --> 00:37:39,447
You're a very, very
talented woman.

748
00:37:42,460 --> 00:37:45,072
And I have a confession to make.

749
00:37:45,096 --> 00:37:46,394
I'm a fan.

750
00:37:48,333 --> 00:37:49,443
Where you from originally?

751
00:37:49,467 --> 00:37:51,612
Arkansas.

752
00:37:51,636 --> 00:37:54,606
That's it. That's it.

753
00:37:56,641 --> 00:38:00,755
Listening to you all this time,

754
00:38:00,779 --> 00:38:04,692
and I just now figured it out.

755
00:38:04,716 --> 00:38:06,260
It's the "r" sound.

756
00:38:06,284 --> 00:38:09,530
People from Arkansas often

757
00:38:09,554 --> 00:38:12,299
add a little "r"
sound to their words.

758
00:38:12,323 --> 00:38:13,601
"Arkansar."

759
00:38:13,625 --> 00:38:16,036
"Sawred-off shotgun."

760
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:20,441
It's a beautiful accent,
but it's unmistakable.

761
00:38:20,465 --> 00:38:27,314
And there your voice
is on Arthur's relief tape.

762
00:38:27,338 --> 00:38:30,618
Why would I call
Arthur's show, hmm?

763
00:38:30,642 --> 00:38:34,455
Well, you called
him late Monday,

764
00:38:34,479 --> 00:38:39,260
knowing it would be taped
and played on Tuesday morning.

765
00:38:39,284 --> 00:38:43,197
So, on Tuesday morning,
you got in Arthur's car,

766
00:38:43,221 --> 00:38:47,868
drove over to Robby Moore's
house, turned on the radio

767
00:38:47,892 --> 00:38:51,605
and waited till you
heard your voice,

768
00:38:51,629 --> 00:38:54,108
knowing that Arthur
would not be on the air live

769
00:38:54,132 --> 00:38:56,310
and therefore would
not have an alibi.

770
00:38:56,334 --> 00:39:00,181
Then you went in the house

771
00:39:00,205 --> 00:39:03,450
with the gun you had
taken from Arthur's party

772
00:39:03,474 --> 00:39:07,275
and shot your old
nemesis to death.

773
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:11,091
<i>Didn't surprise
me she was guilty.</i>

774
00:39:11,115 --> 00:39:12,927
<i>Once that old boy
got his ticker tocking,</i>

775
00:39:12,951 --> 00:39:15,113
<i>it was into the old
jail she was walking.</i>

776
00:39:21,693 --> 00:39:24,004
Sheriff.

777
00:39:24,028 --> 00:39:27,942
I'm entitled to one phone call.

778
00:39:27,966 --> 00:39:29,662
Uh-huh.

779
00:39:30,935 --> 00:39:32,927
Knock yourself out.

780
00:39:45,216 --> 00:39:47,913
Lieutenant Bob Brooks, please.

781
00:39:49,020 --> 00:39:51,065
Bob Brooks, Atlanta P.D.

782
00:39:51,089 --> 00:39:52,132
He'll vouch for me.

783
00:39:52,156 --> 00:39:54,148
Ask him anything you want.

784
00:39:56,527 --> 00:39:58,052
Hello.

785
00:39:59,831 --> 00:40:01,459
Oh, he's not?

786
00:40:03,468 --> 00:40:05,460
Well, thanks anyway.

787
00:40:07,772 --> 00:40:10,150
He's at a roast for Ben Matlock.

788
00:40:10,174 --> 00:40:12,820
I'm Ben Matlock!

789
00:40:12,844 --> 00:40:13,921
Sure.

790
00:40:13,945 --> 00:40:14,922
Uh-uh-uh-uh.

791
00:40:14,946 --> 00:40:16,380
One call.

792
00:41:05,463 --> 00:41:07,841
Pull over! Pull over!

793
00:41:07,865 --> 00:41:09,766
Pu..

794
00:41:16,841 --> 00:41:17,952
Hey, pull over!

795
00:42:07,392 --> 00:42:09,370
Uh-huh.

796
00:42:09,394 --> 00:42:12,806
Uh-huh... Well, good, good.

797
00:42:12,830 --> 00:42:14,475
See you in a few.

798
00:42:15,967 --> 00:42:16,944
Uh, ma'am.

799
00:42:16,968 --> 00:42:18,078
Mm-hmm?

800
00:42:18,102 --> 00:42:20,381
Uh, Deputy Terwilliger
found your car.

801
00:42:20,405 --> 00:42:22,716
Afraid it's been
pretty well totaled.

802
00:42:22,740 --> 00:42:23,917
Totaled?!

803
00:42:23,941 --> 00:42:25,519
My car?

804
00:42:25,543 --> 00:42:28,922
Oh, it was such a darlin' car.

805
00:42:28,946 --> 00:42:30,991
Deputy found your purse, though.

806
00:42:31,015 --> 00:42:32,659
Along with your wallet.

807
00:42:32,683 --> 00:42:35,195
Also found a gun in
the glove compartment.

808
00:42:35,219 --> 00:42:36,530
Looks like your story about

809
00:42:36,554 --> 00:42:38,198
being robbed at
gunpoint checks out.

810
00:42:38,222 --> 00:42:39,500
So we can leave?

811
00:42:39,524 --> 00:42:41,001
Soon as you I.D. the suspect.

812
00:42:41,025 --> 00:42:43,003
Deputy Terwilliger's
bringing him in.

813
00:42:43,027 --> 00:42:46,407
Oh, that's probably
them right now.

814
00:42:46,431 --> 00:42:48,832
- Look... could I...
- Just... just...

815
00:42:52,170 --> 00:42:54,503
Hi, Conrad.

816
00:42:59,277 --> 00:43:00,821
There was one very nice moment

817
00:43:00,845 --> 00:43:02,056
during the whole
miserable experience.

818
00:43:02,080 --> 00:43:06,260
Had nothing to do with
Ben Matlock, of course.

819
00:43:06,284 --> 00:43:10,011
Late one night
<i>in his law office...</i>

820
00:43:16,461 --> 00:43:18,238
Arthur, you can't kiss me here.

821
00:43:18,262 --> 00:43:19,573
All right, I'll kiss you here.

822
00:43:19,597 --> 00:43:21,608
Arthur, no, Ar...

823
00:43:21,632 --> 00:43:22,643
You're making me very nervous.

824
00:43:22,667 --> 00:43:23,844
Since when?

825
00:43:23,868 --> 00:43:26,413
The cleaning crew comes
on Wednesday night.

826
00:43:26,437 --> 00:43:27,414
What time?

827
00:43:27,438 --> 00:43:28,415
10:00.

828
00:43:28,439 --> 00:43:29,817
Got 23 minutes.

829
00:43:52,063 --> 00:43:56,043
She is crazy about me.

830
00:43:56,067 --> 00:43:57,544
Yeah.

831
00:43:57,568 --> 00:44:00,114
Yes, ma'am.

832
00:44:00,138 --> 00:44:04,485
Yes, ma'am, that-that-that-that
sounds fair to me, Mrs. Burnett.

833
00:44:04,509 --> 00:44:05,752
All right. Mm-hmm.

834
00:44:05,776 --> 00:44:06,776
Bye-bye.

835
00:44:08,446 --> 00:44:12,559
Okay, Mr. Murdoch... Matlock.

836
00:44:12,583 --> 00:44:15,863
Since your story checks
out, and since Mrs. Burnett

837
00:44:15,887 --> 00:44:20,000
has agreed not to press charges,
so long as she's adequately

838
00:44:20,024 --> 00:44:22,669
compensated for the
damage you did to her door,

839
00:44:22,693 --> 00:44:24,371
you're all free to go.

840
00:44:24,395 --> 00:44:25,572
Oh.

841
00:44:25,596 --> 00:44:26,573
Oh, that's fine.

842
00:44:26,597 --> 00:44:28,964
How-How's $20 sound?

843
00:44:30,635 --> 00:44:31,745
Try 100.

844
00:44:31,769 --> 00:44:33,046
100?!

845
00:44:33,070 --> 00:44:36,550
100 bucks or she files
criminal and civil charges.

846
00:44:36,574 --> 00:44:40,053
She knows her rights,
too, Mr. Murdoch.

847
00:44:40,077 --> 00:44:41,555
Pay him, Conrad.

848
00:44:41,579 --> 00:44:42,856
Me?!

849
00:44:42,880 --> 00:44:44,691
Well, the man took all my money!

850
00:44:50,254 --> 00:44:52,032
$93... it's all I got.

851
00:44:52,056 --> 00:44:53,649
Close enough.

852
00:44:55,660 --> 00:44:58,372
My car is wrecked,
and so is Conrad's.

853
00:44:58,396 --> 00:45:00,908
And now none of
us have any money.

854
00:45:00,932 --> 00:45:03,710
How are we supposed
to get to Atlanta?

855
00:45:06,170 --> 00:45:07,661
This is good!

856
00:45:13,077 --> 00:45:14,254
Thank you so much.

857
00:45:14,278 --> 00:45:15,322
You're a darlin' person.

858
00:45:15,346 --> 00:45:16,837
Darlin'.

859
00:45:28,859 --> 00:45:30,671
Get your tie.

860
00:45:30,695 --> 00:45:32,406
H-How do I look?

861
00:45:32,430 --> 00:45:33,407
Awful.

862
00:45:33,431 --> 00:45:34,408
Oh.

863
00:45:34,432 --> 00:45:35,695
Okay.

864
00:46:07,398 --> 00:46:09,299
Ladies and gentlemen...

865
00:46:11,302 --> 00:46:14,966
a funny thing happened to
me on the way to this luncheon.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
